**REFUTATION. The strongest fact in the log kills three of the four claims at once, and it is on the lines the brief never quotes: `diag_c83_connect2x2.log:99` and `:110`.**

```
t1 row {'i':1,'name':'Outgoing Handle','is_source':True,'wire':0, ... ,'wire_err':1055, ...}
```

That is the pre-call read of the target terminal. Its `Wire` read **returned error 1055** — "Object reference is invalid" ([NI KB kA00Z0000019L75SAE](https://knowledge.ni.com/KnowledgeArticleDetails?id=kA00Z0000019L75SAE)). The `wire: 0` beside it is the *error path's default*, not a measurement. That 0 is then promoted to a PASS gate at `:101`/`:112` ("the border terminal is BARE before the connect") and re-used as the post-call comparison at `:103`/`:114` ("went BARE -> NON-ZERO — wire 0"). **The entire "no wire was created" observable on the real pair is a failing property read.** It also appears at `:99` *before* `deleted wire w7506` at `:100` — so 1055 on this bed predates the deletion and predates the op edit.

Second structural fact: the S0 "repair (c)" put the new indicator on **`Clear Errors.vi`**'s output (`:29` chain `Invoke#757 → PropNode#241 → PropNode#242 → Clear Errors#399`; `:30` "on Nodes[12] #399"; `:31` "3 hop(s) from the Invoke"). An indicator downstream of Clear Errors is empty *by construction*. So `invoke_err=''` in all eight cells is uninformative, and **explanation (c) is not discharged — it was never tested.** (It does prove the op runs and writes panel indicators, since `invoke_err` lost its poison while `op_err` kept it. So "the op never ran" stays dead.)

**CLAIM 1 — wrong, three confounds.** (i) The two arms run *different ops*: trivial = a scratch copy of `OpConnect_v0` (Invoke Nodes[11] #99, `:38`), real = `OpFsInnerTunnelConnect_v0` (Invoke Nodes[17] #757, `:27`). Different VI, different surrounding graph. (ii) Different regime: two bare Property-node terminals on one flat diagram vs. a flat-sequence inner-tunnel terminal and a loop-border terminal, which may not even share an owning diagram. (iii) **The 2×2 never varied polarity.** A1/A2 and B1/B2 only move which end hosts the Invoke; the pair is source→sink legal either way (`:48`), so a method that infers direction from `Is Source` passes all four cells regardless of roles. *Alternative:* `Connect Wire` ignores which end hosts the Invoke whenever exactly one end is a source, and the real pair fails because **both ends are sources** — `:99` says the loop terminal `is_source: True`, and nobody has ever printed `Is Source` for FSIT `#7488`. *Falsifier:* a trivial cell wiring two *sinks* (or two sources) that also fails silently — then polarity, not role, is decisive and the 2×2 proves nothing about the real pair. Also: `Auto Route?` default is documented **False** ([LabVIEW Wiki, Terminal/Connect Wire](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Terminal_class/Connect_Wire_method)), so (b) was never a live hypothesis; `Wire Source` is a **GObject** refnum, "the original source of the wire".

**CLAIM 2 — wrong, and it is the junk that matters.** "Auto Route TRUE changed nothing" is not a finding about the method when the only channels that could show a change are (a) a 1055-failing `Wire` read and (b) a `wire_delta` that stayed 0 while `junk_Invoke=1` on *every* real-pair call and 0 on every trivial cell (`:102`, `:113` vs `:49`–`:67`). Something mutated the bed diagram each time and the census counted zero wires changed — **so the census does not see everything the call does**, and an unexplained extra `Invoke` node is a partial-execution signature nobody has accounted for. Note also `is_broken=False` in the same RESULT line: something answered an `Is Broken?` query. *Alternative:* the wire was created on a nested diagram (frame) that the 173-row census does not count, and the loop-side `Wire` property is simply unreadable on this terminal over this COM path. *Falsifier:* a post-call `Wire` read that returns a uid — or the same 1055 — on the FSIT side rather than the loop side.

**CLAIM 3 — I do NOT think the 1055 is the machine's real answer, but the run cannot settle it, and "exactly two things changed" is false.** Against the deletion story: 1055 is already produced at `:99`, pre-delete; and c82 resolved 7488/7468 on the same bed. The better artefact story is not the added indicator (an indicator downstream of Clear Errors cannot create an upstream 1055) — it is **the poison**. If the poisoning pass writes by label and touches anything feeding `UID to GObject Reference.vi`, the op runs with UID **0**, `To More Specific Class` throws 1055 on a null ref, and that null ref propagates to the next Property Nodes — reproducing the exact signature ("1055 in `UID to GObject Reference.vi`" + "1055: Property Node" on three stages) with `term_uid=0`/`uid_back=0` being *computed*, not left over. *Falsifier / the single settling observation you asked for:* **resolve UID 7488 to a GObject ref and read one harmless property (`Class Name`, `Owner`) twice in one script — once on an untouched copy, once after deleting w7506 — with the op at its pre-repair md5 `c0d5efe3…` and nothing poisoned.** Both resolve ⇒ artefact. Second fails only ⇒ the machine's real answer and the tunnel must be re-created, not re-attached.

**CLAIM 4 — the diagnosis is plausible but the diagnostic cannot see the competing cause.** `:82` enumerates *unwired sinks only*; `#757` is fully wired there, and `(241,'reference')`/`(187,'reference')` are consistent with the deleted nets w572/w1337 having had other branches. But ExecState 0 has a second candidate the list is blind to: **a broken wire**. `reference` on a Terminal-class Invoke is typed `Terminal`; `Wire Source` is the wide `GObject`. The swap sends `#183.'LeftTerm'` into the narrow input (`:80`) — if `LeftTerm` is statically `GObject`, that is a downcast and LabVIEW breaks wire 1664 while "verified on both ends" still passes, because endpoint-uid equality checks neither type nor identity. *Alternative:* ExecState 0 is the type break, and re-branching #241/#187 leaves it broken. *Falsifier:* `Wire.Is Broken?` returning True on 1660 or 1664. A second warning: re-branching a consumer onto whichever source is convenient can make the copy legal while changing *which object it reads* — legal ≠ equivalent (CLAUDE.md rule 1a).

**RANKED TESTS — one script, scratch copies, no new op, no new method id, nothing written to the bed.**

1. **Move the error tap one hop up.** `create_indicator` on Invoke **#757 `error out` t3 (wire 1027)**, *upstream* of #241/#242/#399, on a scratch copy of the op; re-run one real-pair cell with poisoning OFF. This reads 6349C03's own verdict and is the only test that can answer claims 1–3 together. Everything so far has read a cleared error.
2. **Endpoint audit, read-only, before any connect:** for FSIT `#7488` *and* loop `23032/Terminals[1]`, print `Class Name`, `Owner` (→ owning-diagram uid), `Is Source`, `Wire` — and **print the error code of every read, never a defaulted 0**. Settles same-diagram, settles source-vs-source, and re-runs itself on an undeleted vs deleted copy to settle claim 3.
3. **`Wire.Is Broken?` (6371004, already built — `docs/NAMES.md:902-911`) on wires 1660 and 1664** of the swapped copy. Separates claim 4's "missing consumers" from a type break. CLAUDE.md's own rule applies: this class of failure has now been explained by inference twice and the reader exists.
4. **Identify the junk `Invoke`** minted per real-pair call (class, owner, label) before it is deleted.

Two addressing caveats to carry forward: "uid **echo** 23032 MATCH" confirms the node, never that `Terminals[1]` is the shift-register outer terminal — and `'Outgoing Handle'` is not a shift-register terminal name I can source anywhere. If the index is wrong, every claim above is moot.

**What would change my mind:** test 1 returning "no error, wire reference valid" — that would make the method innocent and the observable guilty; or test 2 showing both endpoints on the same diagram with opposite `Is Source` *and* a clean 1055-free `Wire` read, which would put explanation (c) back as the whole story.

